// OpenSessionWithBodyWithResponse Open or resume the session for a workspace
//
// Resolves a workspace directory to its session and returns it, creating the session the first time the directory is opened. This is the only REST operation that creates a workspace session, so a controller starts with none and a client must name the workspace it wants. `POST /sessions/system` creates the system session. It takes no `X-Session-ID` header; use the returned `session.id` as `X-Session-ID` on later REST calls and as `metadata.sessionId` on the WebSocket. The response also carries it in the `X-Session-ID` response header.
//
// The path is resolved through its symbolic links, so opening the same directory again by any of its names resumes the same session. All entry points targeting that directory use its session.
//
// Each workspace session has one agent space.
//
// - For a workspace with no session yet, `agentSpace` picks the agent space. Leaving it out gives the deployment default (`default` in `GET /agent-spaces`).
// - For a workspace with a session, leaving `agentSpace` out or naming its current space reuses the session. Naming a different one returns 409 `AGENT_SPACE_CONFLICT`, whose message names the workspace's current agent space. Use `POST /session/reconfigure` to move the workspace on purpose.
//
// Errors, none of which creates a session:
//
// - 400 `VALIDATION_FAILED`: the body is missing or malformed, `workspace` is empty, or the path is not a directory.
// - 403 `AGENT_SPACE_NOT_ALLOWED`: `agentSpace` names an agent space the deployment does not offer. This is checked first, even when the workspace already has a session.
// - 403 `WORKSPACE_NOT_ALLOWED`: the path is outside every configured workspace root (before or after resolving symbolic links), or the workspace is, contains, or sits inside the controller's state directory, or is or contains its configuration directory. The message never names a root.
// - 404 `WORKSPACE_NOT_FOUND`: the path is beneath a configured root but the directory does not exist.
// - 409 `AGENT_SPACE_CONFLICT`: see above.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /sessions/open (the `OpenSession` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) OpenSessionWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*OpenSessionResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.OpenSessionWithBody(ctx, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseOpenSessionResponse(rsp)
}

